                    "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
                    "body": "> Let me ask a follow-up question - can quickfix lists be persisted?\r\n\r\nIf it can be done with built-in sessions (which it doesn't look like possible), the only possible way is to write custom save/restore logic and use it in mentioned hooks. For example, [this answer](https://vi.stackexchange.com/a/38609) suggests using separate file (which I'd expect should be named the same as session for the purpose of this use case).",
                    "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Let me ask a follow-up question - can quickfix lists be persisted?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">If it can be done with built-in sessions (which it doesn't look like possible), the only possible way is to write custom save/restore logic and use it in mentioned hooks. For example, <a href=\"https://vi.stackexchange.com/a/38609\" rel=\"nofollow\">this answer</a> suggests using separate file (which I'd expect should be named the same as session for the purpose of this use case).</p>",
                    "bodyText": "Let me ask a follow-up question - can quickfix lists be persisted?\n\nIf it can be done with built-in sessions (which it doesn't look like possible), the only possible way is to write custom save/restore logic and use it in mentioned hooks. For example, this answer suggests using separate file (which I'd expect should be named the same as session for the purpose of this use case).",
